cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PhantomLoad: measuring the gap between enrolled and delivered demand response capacity"
message: "If you use this repository or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
    affiliation: "independent"
version: 0.4.2
repository-code: "https://github.com/ASM-21/phantomload"
date-released: "2026-10-04"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - demand response
  - grid reliability
  - utility economics
  - capacity market
  - EIA-861
abstract: >-
  US utilities realise about 39 percent of the demand response peak reduction
  they report as available, a gap that has held for twelve years. Using
  EIA-861 utility-level files for 2016 to 2018, which reconcile to the national
  series to the megawatt, this work decomposes that gap into three components
  with different mechanisms. Capacity reported at exactly zero rotates between
  years and concentrates where utilities dispatch for themselves, which reads as
  available but uncalled. Capacity with no reported outcome is not wholesale
  bookkeeping. Whether partial delivery, about half the gap, responds to system
  conditions is specification-dependent. Nearly a third of positive reports set
  actual exactly equal to potential, a convention that is concentrated in small
  programs and persists within utilities, and that produced an apparent size
  gradient this work first reported and then withdrew.
  Priced against PJM's published cost of new entry, demand response is cheaper
  in six of eight defensible pairings of accounting bases. The public record
  does not support reading the gap as waste, and the work states what data
  would settle it.
